{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DVXbnyWuTxxJ85zNTjrnkb","type":"tool_result","content":"{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Minimal removal sets (verified by exact Bradley-Terry refit) that flip pairwise-comparison leaderboard rankings, and how the budget differs between crowd-vote (Chatbot Arena), expert/LLM-judge (MT-Bench) and sports (NBA Elo) comparison data.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Bradley-Terry maximum-likelihood fitting on pairwise outcome counts\",\n    \"first-order influence / approximate maximum influence perturbation for data-dropping robustness\",\n    \"greedy removal search with exact-refit verification, prefix bisection and swap polish\",\n    \"cell-aggregated (pair-count) weighted refits with relaxed removal search\",\n    \"stationarity-LP over target ratings for minimal removal sets\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"random-removal baseline\",\n    \"permuted/shuffled-outcome control\",\n    \"influence-function and AMIP-style dropping-data robustness metrics\",\n    \"vote-rigging and adversarial manipulation of crowdsourced leaderboards\",\n    \"bootstrap/confidence-interval based ranking uncertainty\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"Chatbot Arena human preference (55k / 140k)\",\n    \"MT-Bench human expert and GPT-4 judgments\",\n    \"NBA historical Elo game records\",\n    \"LLM-judge judgments on Arena battles\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [\n    \"row-level influence greedy to cell-aggregated weighted refits\",\n    \"myopic first-order search to relaxed implicit-gradient / LP-based target-rating search with exact rounding and fallback\"\n  ],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"large crowd-vote tables flip with a tiny removal fraction while the small expert-judgment table resisted flipping within the removal cap in the search phase\",\n    \"influence-guided removal is far tighter than random removal, and permuted-outcome controls need larger budgets than real data\",\n    \"three search variants tied on the search-stage score, so the exact-refit-verified greedy is already near the achievable budget on the large crowd table\",\n    \"flip budget depends on the score gap relative to noise and on the evaluation design\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Bradley-Terry model paired comparisons maximum likelihood estimation ranking\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Defines the scoring model refit exactly in every flip-budget verification; needs the original model paper and standard MLE/fitting references.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"approximate maximum influence perturbation dropping small fraction of data robustness\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Closest methodological antecedent: first-order influence to find a small data subset whose removal changes a conclusion, which the greedy search re-linearises and verifies exactly.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"understanding black-box predictions influence functions\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source of the influence-function machinery underlying the greedy removal ranking; reviewers will expect it as the first-order comparator.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Chatbot Arena open platform evaluating LLMs human preference\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Describes the crowd-vote data (55k/140k battles) and the Bradley-Terry ranking pipeline being stress-tested.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"judging LLM-as-a-judge MT-Bench human expert pairwise judgments\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Provides the expert-judgment and GPT-4 judge comparison data, the small contrasting evaluation design with only a few models.\"\n    },\n    {\n43:Leaderboards that aggregate ``which of A and B is better'' judgments into Bradley--Terry (BT) ratings are used to choose models, to announce releases, and to compare research systems. The BT model and its many extensions are decades old \\citep{Davidson1970OnET,Davidson1969MultivariatePC,Leonard1977AnAB}, and the statistical properties of its estimates under sampling noise are increasingly well understood \\citep{Simons1999AsymptoticsWT,Gao2021UncertaintyQI,Li2021ThePO}. What is less often reported is a complementary, \\emph{combinatorial} question: how much of the underlying comparison table has to change, whether through noise or deliberate action, before a published rank order reverses?\n45:We make this question operational. Given a comparison table and one adjacent pair of rated participants, the \\emph{removal budget} is the smallest number of rows whose deletion makes an exact refit of the same BT objective rank the lower participant above the upper one. Deletion is a deliberately weak perturbation: it neither inserts nor relabels votes, and it is closely related to the ``dropping a little data'' robustness question of \\citet{Broderick2020AnAF}. Because every proposed deletion set is checked by an exact refit, any reported budget is a certificate that a reversal is attainable with that many rows, and therefore an upper bound on the true minimum.\n53:\\paragraph{Contributions.} (1) A precisely specified, evaluator-verified removal-budget protocol with comparators and controls, whose budgets are certificates rather than estimates. (2) Measured budgets for four held-out cases on two evaluation designs, with the crowd-versus-sports contrast and its confounds spelled out. (3) A negative result: first-order influence ordering with exact-refit bisection matches the more elaborate configuration everywhere we measured. (4) An explicit scope statement: the claims concern the declared cases only, not confidence-interval-based rank changes, adversarial insertion or vote rigging, other rating models, or causal effects of evaluation design.\n62:\\paragraph{Scope of the bibliography.} Work on adversarial manipulation of crowd-vote leaderboards, on evaluation practices of arena-style platforms, and on influence functions for black-box predictions is relevant background. It is not part of the verified reference set for this submission, so we do not position our measurements against it and make no claim of novelty relative to it.\n96:The perturbation scale and the cross-dataset comparison are choices, so we state the reasons for them. \\emph{Deletion only:} every remaining row is a genuine observation, so a flip cannot be blamed on fabricated data, and an exact refit turns each deletion set into a checkable certificate. Insertion, relabelling and vote rigging would require an adversary model that we do not specify. \\emph{Adjacent pairs:} if a removal set puts $\\lo$ above $\\hi$ and the original ranking placed other participants between them, then at least one adjacent pair in that chain must also have reversed. The smallest budget over adjacent pairs therefore lower-bounds the budget of any non-adjacent reversal, and adjacent pairs are the cheapest rank events. \\emph{Top and mid:} the top pair is the position that leaderboards publicise, and the mid pair probes a typical interior position. \\emph{A 2\\% cap:} it fixes a common tolerance for how much of a table noise or an actor could plausibly alter and makes scores comparable across tables of different size. It is a convention of the evaluator, not an estimate of any real process, and we did not vary it; all held-out budgets sit well below it except the sports top pair, which uses 24\\% of its allowance. % @evidence[[derived/final_stats.json#cases.F3.k_over_cap]]\n245:\\paragraph{What it does not support.} (i) \\emph{No causal claim about evaluation design.} Crowd votes and sports are confounded with table size, participants, era, outcome granularity and tie structure; one table per design means the contrast cannot be separated from table identity. (ii) \\emph{No claim about expert or model-judge designs on held-out data.} The only expert-design evidence is a six-model development table with no flip within the cap and an unknown cause; no model-judge data were evaluated. (iii) \\emph{No superiority claim.} The studied configuration equals influence-only removal in every case, and the starting baseline was not run on held-out tables. The qualified outcome of the held-out evaluation certifies that the evaluation completed with its controls passing; it neither establishes that the configuration is better nor extends findings beyond four cases. (iv) \\emph{No rank-uncertainty or manipulation claim.} A flip here is a reversal of a point-estimate order for one adjacent pair. We examine neither confidence-interval-based rank sets, nor insertion, label flipping or rigging by an adversary, nor other rating models; an adversary can usually do more than delete rows, and a platform's published rank may rest on other statistics. (v) \\emph{Limited uncertainty.} Two identical episodes, 20 random draws, and four cases do not support significance statements, and we compute no intervals over data sets.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c65d0b3f-b02e-43cf-8f32-f0979b82e225","uuid":"eea2e32c-d742-4d29-bd8e-78cd33a6e7ad","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:10:31.056Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Minimal removal sets (verified by exact Bradley-Terry refit) that flip pairwise-comparison leaderboard rankings, and how the budget differs between crowd-vote (Chatbot Arena), expert/LLM-judge (MT-Bench) and sports (NBA Elo) comparison data.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Bradley-Terry maximum-likelihood fitting on pairwise outcome counts\",\n    \"first-order influence / approximate maximum influence perturbation for data-dropping robustness\",\n    \"greedy removal search with exact-refit verification, prefix bisection and swap polish\",\n    \"cell-aggregated (pair-count) weighted refits with relaxed removal search\",\n   